# First DCM Identity by Direct Multiplication (Planar \(z\)-axis rotation)

Goal: explicitly compute \(C_i^{\,B}\dot C_B^{\,i}\) for the planar DCM and show it equals \( [\omega]^\times \).

---

## 1) Write down the DCMs

Given (from the slide) the inertial-to-body DCM:

\[
C_i^{\,B}=
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta & \sin\theta & 0\\
-\sin\theta & \cos\theta & 0\\
0&0&1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

The inverse (transpose) is the body-to-inertial DCM:

\[
C_B^{\,i} = (C_i^{\,B})^T =
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta & 0\\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta & 0\\
0&0&1
\end{bmatrix}
\]

---

## 2) Differentiate \(C_B^{\,i}\) entry-by-entry

Use the chain rule:

\[
\frac{d}{dt}\cos\theta = -\dot\theta \sin\theta,
\qquad
\frac{d}{dt}\sin\theta = \dot\theta \cos\theta
\]

Therefore:

\[
\dot C_B^{\,i}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-\dot\theta\sin\theta & -\dot\theta\cos\theta & 0\\
\dot\theta\cos\theta & -\dot\theta\sin\theta & 0\\
0&0&0
\end{bmatrix}
\]

---

## 3) Multiply \(C_i^{\,B}\dot C_B^{\,i}\) explicitly

Compute:

\[
C_i^{\,B}\dot C_B^{\,i}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta & \sin\theta & 0\\
-\sin\theta & \cos\theta & 0\\
0&0&1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
-\dot\theta\sin\theta & -\dot\theta\cos\theta & 0\\
\dot\theta\cos\theta & -\dot\theta\sin\theta & 0\\
0&0&0
\end{bmatrix}
\]

### Entry (1,1)

\[
(\cos\theta)(-\dot\theta\sin\theta)+(\sin\theta)(\dot\theta\cos\theta)
=
-\dot\theta\sin\theta\cos\theta+\dot\theta\sin\theta\cos\theta
=0
\]

### Entry (1,2)

\[
(\cos\theta)(-\dot\theta\cos\theta)+(\sin\theta)(-\dot\theta\sin\theta)
=
-\dot\theta(\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta)
=
-\dot\theta
\]

### Entry (2,1)

\[
(-\sin\theta)(-\dot\theta\sin\theta)+(\cos\theta)(\dot\theta\cos\theta)
=
\dot\theta(\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta)
=
\dot\theta
\]

### Entry (2,2)

\[
(-\sin\theta)(-\dot\theta\cos\theta)+(\cos\theta)(-\dot\theta\sin\theta)
=
\dot\theta\sin\theta\cos\theta-\dot\theta\sin\theta\cos\theta
=0
\]

All entries involving the 3rd row/column are zero because the last row/column of \(\dot C_B^{\,i}\) is zero.

So the product is:

\[
C_i^{\,B}\dot C_B^{\,i}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -\dot\theta & 0\\
\dot\theta & 0 & 0\\
0&0&0
\end{bmatrix}
\]

---

## 4) Recognize this as \( [\omega]^\times \)

For rotation about the \(z\)-axis:

\[
\boldsymbol\omega=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0\\
\dot\theta
\end{bmatrix}
\]

The cross-product matrix \( [\omega]^\times \) is:

\[
[\omega]^\times =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -\omega_z & \omega_y\\
\omega_z & 0 & -\omega_x\\
-\omega_y & \omega_x & 0
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -\dot\theta & 0\\
\dot\theta & 0 & 0\\
0&0&0
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
C_i^{\,B}\dot C_B^{\,i} = [\omega]^\times
}
\]